+#
+# Five failures so far, and every one of them is worth keeping because every one
+# of them is a trap that looks like a tuning problem and is not:
+#
+#   1. Built from ISO-CONTOURS OF NOISE. Contours do close into loops, which was
+#      the intent, but they scatter isotropically and their WIDTH tracks the
+#      local gradient - flat parts of the field give fat bands, steep parts give
+#      hairlines. It rendered as blobs of uneven thickness and read as water
+#      stains. The fix was a Wave in SAW profile: constant gradient means every
+#      stroke is identically wide, and parallel bands supply the baseline and
+#      repetition that make an eye call something writing.
+#
+#   2. Constant width was right; constant LENGTH was not. Rows of identical
+#      dashes read as machine stitching - perforation, hazard tape, an LED
+#      strip. Same pen, different words: the width is still fixed and the LENGTH
+#      now varies about 4x, the rows are decorrelated so they cannot column up
+#      into a quilted grid, and per-stroke brightness varies so the line
+#      shimmers unevenly instead of firing as one bar.
+#
+#   3. It also out-read the gem at 1.90 and climbed to the knee. Both are the
+#      same mistake - spending the effect's budget on AREA. See VOW_EMIT and
+#      VOW_Z_OFF.
+#
+#   4. EMISSION-ONLY. A wide halo was already there and nobody could see it, and
+#      the reason is not that it was too dim: emission is added over the surface
+#      without the surface changing, so the cloth around a stroke was still the
+#      same cloth and the strokes read as painted on. The fix is that the halo
+#      now also lifts BASE COLOR, so the fabric near a stroke is lighter fabric.
+#      See VOW_ALB.
+#
+#   5. TOO MUCH OF IT. With the mechanism finally right the whole thing lost on
+#      AREA instead: five courses at ~50% duty under a 15 mm halo covered more
+#      of the icon than the gem did, and each course was lit for longer than it
+#      was dark, so it welded shut into hoop-banding. Note that 4 and 5 pull in
+#      opposite directions - 4 says the light must reach into the cloth, 5 says
